From: psigod <psigod@example.com>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 21:00:00 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] linux: keep the kernel's charging status

A negative current_now turned a charging battery into a discharging one.
On a tablet booting from a 500 mA USB port the battery drains for about
20 s while the system starts, so with an empty battery UPower took the
critical action on every boot. Android shows the charger's status there;
do the same and keep the guess for the other states.
---
--- a/src/linux/up-device-supply-battery.c
+++ b/src/linux/up-device-supply-battery.c
@@ -519,10 +519,16 @@
 	/* For some battery solutions, for example axp20x-battery, the kernel reports
 	 * status = charging but the battery actually discharges when connecting a
 	 * charger. Upower reports the battery is "discharging" when current_now is
-	 * found and is a negative value as long as the battery isn't fully charged.*/
+	 * found and is a negative value as long as the battery isn't fully charged.
+	 *
+	 * Not when the kernel says charging: like Android, show what the charger
+	 * does. A system that draws more than a weak charger gives (booting from
+	 * a 500 mA port) drains the battery for a while, and taking that as
+	 * discharging powered an empty tablet off on every boot.*/
 	values.state = up_device_supply_get_state (native);
 
 	if (values.state != UP_DEVICE_STATE_FULLY_CHARGED &&
+	    values.state != UP_DEVICE_STATE_CHARGING &&
 	    g_udev_device_get_sysfs_attr_as_double_uncached (native, "current_now") < 0.0)
 		values.state = UP_DEVICE_STATE_DISCHARGING;
 
